recordings reads the daemon's store: what 'ley record' wrote, newest first.
A recording is one directory of files -- 'ley recordings path' says where, so
'open -R "$(ley recordings path <id>)"' reveals it in Finder.

'ley recordings delete' removes one. The store is a plain directory, so a
recording deleted there in Finder is gone too, and the daemon does not have
to be told; the daemon drops the oldest when the store passes its cap
(leylined --recordings-cap).

--json prints a ListResourcesResponse.

Usage:
  ley recordings [flags]
  ley recordings [command]

Examples:
  ley recordings                       # everything kept, newest first
  ley recordings --kind audio          # just the WAVs
  ley recordings --freq 146.52         # one frequency
  ley recordings show job_01J...       # the manifest: parts, gaps, the radio
  open -R "$(ley recordings path job_01J...)"
  ley recordings delete job_01J...     # asks first; --yes does not

Available Commands:
  delete      Delete a recording: every part and its manifest
  path        Print where a recording is on this machine
  show        Print one recording's manifest

Flags:
      --freq string    only recordings of this frequency or preset, e.g. 146.52
  -h, --help           help for recordings
      --kind string    only audio or iq recordings
      --limit int      at most this many rows, e.g. 20 (default: all of them)
      --since string   only recordings started more recently than this, e.g. 1h, 30m, 2d

Global Flags:
      --ascii           draw with ASCII only (no box, bar or tree glyphs)
      --color string    colour output: auto, always or never (default "auto")
      --json            machine output: proto3 JSON (NDJSON for streams)
      --socket string   daemon socket path (default: user daemon UDS, $LEYLINE_SOCKET)

Use "ley recordings [command] --help" for more information about a command.
